{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Hannah, sorry you're stuck. Your account is active and isn't locked, and it's correctly set up to ask you for a new password. That means your temporary password is working. The error usually comes from the new password you're choosing, or from where you're changing it. Please try this: 1) Change it at the Windows sign-in screen on your company laptop, not on a web page, while you're in the office or connected to VPN. 2) Make your new password at least 14 characters long. 3) Don't reuse a recent password. 4) Check that Caps Lock is off and your keyboard language is right, and watch for look-alike characters (l vs I, 0 vs O). If it still fails, please reply with the exact error message and tell me where you were signing in (Windows screen or a web page) so we can take the next step. Please don't include any password in your reply.",
  "internal_note": "KB-003 checks: Account active, must_change_password=true, failed_login_count=0, not locked or blocked, VPN access on. password_last_set 2026-10-09 14:45 UTC (temporary password issued). There are no failed sign-ins in the 14-day logs, so the temp password itself isn't being rejected. The failure is at the change step, most likely the 14-character minimum or password history, or she's changing it on the web or off-network. Laptop BL-LT-0112 is online and checked in at 2026-10-09 14:33 UTC, no warnings. No unlock or reset needed right now. Asked the user for the exact error and where she's signing in. Also noted: her sign-ins show mfa=none, so check Duo enrollment as a follow-up. Next step: if she follows the guidance on the Windows screen on network and it still fails, issue a new temp password per KB-002. If no password works at all, escalate per KB-003.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-003",
    "KB-002"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
